00:00:34.969 --> 00:00:36.549
[MUSIC]

00:00:36.549 --> 00:00:42.019
&gt;&gt; Imagine what it would be like to be at
your best every time you communicated,

00:00:42.019 --> 00:00:46.810
alert, focused, engaged,
and with minimal fear.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.432
Today, using research from neuroscience,

00:00:49.432 --> 00:00:53.919
we'll explore how you can hack your
communication to maximize your impact.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.344
I'm Matt Abrahams, and

00:00:56.344 --> 00:01:00.960
I teach strategic communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.640
Welcome to Think Fast Talk Smart,
the podcast.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:09.938
I am really looking forward to speaking
with Andrew Huberman, who is a professor

00:01:09.938 --> 00:01:14.740
in the neurobiology department at
Stanford University School of Medicine.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:20.172
Andrew's research focuses on understanding
the brain mechanisms controlling anxiety,

00:01:20.172 --> 00:01:22.719
cognition and performance under stress.

00:01:22.719 --> 00:01:27.915
Additionally, Andrew works diligently to
bring neuroscience research to the public,

00:01:27.915 --> 00:01:30.133
through his teaching, his videos and

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:34.166
his Huberman lab podcast which
needs to be in everyone's playlist.

00:01:34.166 --> 00:01:37.670
Thanks for being here, Andrew,
should we get started?

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.260
&gt;&gt; Delighted to be here.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:43.400
&gt;&gt; Like me, I know you're
fascinated by fear and its impact.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.442
As we all know, communicating in front
of others in high stakes situations can

00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:49.600
certainly involve fear.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.110
From a neurologic point of view,
what's going on.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:56.259
What happens to our voice, our speech and
our hand movements when we get nervous?

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:04.829
&gt;&gt; So when we get nervous, we are entering
a state which is perfectly natural but

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:11.350
reflects one station along what's
called the autonomic continuum.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:15.737
And really the autonomic
continuum can be conceptualized as

00:02:15.737 --> 00:02:19.521
a continuum between states
of very high alertness,

00:02:19.521 --> 00:02:24.960
think maximum excitement or
fear all the way down to deep sleep.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.910
So our states of mind and
body are not discrete entities,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.550
they are along this continuum,
this autonomic continuum.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.742
So when we are excited or we are nervous,
we have a number of physiological

00:02:38.742 --> 00:02:45.160
responses that are purely autonomic,
meaning they're purely automatic.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.430
The most important thing to
realize is that it is a continuum.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.172
It has some features that
are autonomic meaning automatic.

00:02:54.172 --> 00:02:59.147
And that there's no difference between
the physiological response to something

00:02:59.147 --> 00:03:00.949
that you're excited about,

00:03:00.949 --> 00:03:05.660
and something that you're nervous about or
that you're dreading.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.910
And so there's an additional component
there that we need to consider.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.671
But the most important thing above all,
and

00:03:11.671 --> 00:03:16.445
I guess if people are going to take away
anything from our conversation today,

00:03:16.445 --> 00:03:21.444
it's that despite these responses being
automatic, there are direct control

00:03:21.444 --> 00:03:25.713
points through which we can control
the autonomic nervous system.

00:03:25.713 --> 00:03:28.889
Meaning that we can dial down
the level of alertness, or

00:03:28.889 --> 00:03:30.790
increase the level of calmness.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:32.830
Those actually turn out to
be two different things.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:37.062
So while it's called the autonomic nervous
system and the autonomic continuum,

00:03:37.062 --> 00:03:38.421
it's a bit of a misnomer.

00:03:38.421 --> 00:03:43.211
Because there are two specific levers or
entry points that we all have from

00:03:43.211 --> 00:03:48.246
birth that require essentially no
learning, you have to know what they are.

00:03:48.246 --> 00:03:52.137
But that will allow people to
control voice, level of alertness,

00:03:52.137 --> 00:03:54.092
level of shaking in their hands.

00:03:54.092 --> 00:03:58.451
So it's a quite remarkable system in
that it has this asymmetry, autonomic on

00:03:58.451 --> 00:04:02.556
the one hand but with very powerful
entry points for control on the other.

00:04:02.556 --> 00:04:06.909
&gt;&gt; I want to get back to the notion
that you brought up about

00:04:06.909 --> 00:04:11.730
how we experience the physiological
arousal that we get.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:15.700
You said, in some cases,
it's due to anxiety and fear.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.540
In other cases, it's due to excitement.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.840
Part of that, I think, has to do with
how we label what we're experiencing.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.939
And I know that there's some
research from my academic field,

00:04:24.939 --> 00:04:28.506
that says one way to help manage anxiety
is just to work on how you perceive

00:04:28.506 --> 00:04:31.040
the physiological symptoms
that you're having.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.507
So, if you say, hey, even though my heart
rate is going up, I'm getting a little

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:37.980
sweaty and shaky, it's because I'm excited
to share the information I'm sharing,

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:41.280
versus my goodness, everybody is
looking at me and I'm feeling nervous.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.450
So part of it, I think,
has to do with how we frame the situation.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:46.760
Does that ring true?

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.746
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, when we are in a state of
alertness, whether because of excitement

00:04:52.746 --> 00:04:58.014
or fear, the sympathetic division of the
autonomic nervous system, let's just call

00:04:58.014 --> 00:05:03.670
it the alertness system, deploys a hormone
from our adrenal glands, adrenaline.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:07.533
And it deploys the equivalent chemical in
the brain where it's called epinephrine.

00:05:07.533 --> 00:05:10.171
It's actually the same
chemically identical structure,

00:05:10.171 --> 00:05:12.704
but called two different things
because neuroscientists and

00:05:12.704 --> 00:05:14.938
physiologists like to
make things complicated.

00:05:14.938 --> 00:05:16.065
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Right.

00:05:16.065 --> 00:05:21.439
&gt;&gt; Not simple, but
the role of adrenaline slash epinephrine

00:05:21.439 --> 00:05:28.220
is to create agitation in the body and
to create focus in the mind.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.266
And this is an important
concept because that agitation

00:05:32.266 --> 00:05:36.580
makes it harder to be still,
which is sort of a duh, right?

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.700
That's the definition of agitation.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.348
But it was designed to physically move us,
so

00:05:42.348 --> 00:05:45.900
that we would be biased toward ambulation.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:51.066
We're biased toward shifting
from one position to a new one.

00:05:51.066 --> 00:05:55.354
And so one of the toughest things for
many people is to tolerate that level of

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:58.360
adrenaline or
alertness when they have to be still.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.457
The simplest example I can give of this
that I think most people will be familiar

00:06:02.457 --> 00:06:05.251
with is, in the days where
we congregated in person,

00:06:05.251 --> 00:06:08.605
there's this traditional practice
of going around the room and

00:06:08.605 --> 00:06:12.510
introducing yourself, and
saying something about what you do.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:15.991
And most people actually find
that to be very stressful,

00:06:15.991 --> 00:06:18.675
especially toward the end of the line.

00:06:18.675 --> 00:06:19.935
Now why would that be, right?

00:06:19.935 --> 00:06:21.995
Most people know their name and
can say their name.

00:06:21.995 --> 00:06:24.980
Most people know what they do and
can say that, it's anything but

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.345
a high stress circumstance.

00:06:26.345 --> 00:06:29.854
And occasionally, there's some
social pressure where if someone's

00:06:29.854 --> 00:06:33.304
very funny before us or they say
something in a particularly nice way,

00:06:33.304 --> 00:06:36.530
then we feel some additional
pressure to do that as well.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:40.310
But it really has more to do with the fact
that when we're in a room listening

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.030
to somebody,

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:45.052
we're comfortable with the fact that we're
not going to speak or walk or do much.

00:06:45.052 --> 00:06:47.693
And we can just sit there and
write or listen or text or

00:06:47.693 --> 00:06:49.144
whatever it is we have to do.

00:06:49.144 --> 00:06:54.296
As we are called on to say something,
the reason it's easier

00:06:54.296 --> 00:07:00.980
to do early in the line is because we
are holding on to a reverberatory circuit.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:04.891
There are circuits in our brain that
anticipate action and prepare us for

00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:05.810
action.

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:10.220
And the longer we keep that in check,
the more challenging it becomes.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:14.864
When we are trying to withhold action,
but we're preparing for

00:07:14.864 --> 00:07:18.047
action, there's a lot of reverberatory,

00:07:18.047 --> 00:07:22.962
excuse me, activity in our nervous
system and it feels like stres.

00:07:22.962 --> 00:07:25.587
Getting ready to go up
to the podium is tough.

00:07:25.587 --> 00:07:28.449
When we get up to the podium,
many people, including myself,

00:07:28.449 --> 00:07:30.717
find that if we rock back and
forth a little bit, or

00:07:30.717 --> 00:07:33.850
we can engage some movement in our body,
suddenly we start to relax.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:39.410
And that's because adrenaline slash
epinephrine was designed to move us.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:43.570
And it wasn't designed to move us in
response to incoming large predators,

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:46.250
it was, but
that's not its primary function.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:49.996
Its primary function was to move us
from whatever position we're in to a new

00:07:49.996 --> 00:07:54.159
position, sometimes towards things,
sometimes away from things, depending on

00:07:54.159 --> 00:07:57.799
whether or not we want the experience or
we want to avoid the experience.

00:07:57.799 --> 00:08:01.473
But the actual inner experience,
what we call interoception,

00:08:01.473 --> 00:08:05.069
our perception of our internal
landscape is identical For

00:08:05.069 --> 00:08:09.045
something that we want to approach
versus we want to move away from,

00:08:09.045 --> 00:08:13.310
absolutely identical below that
from the neck down that is.

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:14.180
&gt;&gt; Really interesting.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:20.310
So, if you can reframe the physiological
response, you can see it very differently.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:25.150
And I find fascinating that when
we see somebody who is nervous.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:27.700
Moving one way versus the other way,
as an audience member,

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:29.260
we have very different perception.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.330
So if somebody steps up on a stage, and

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.244
then takes a step back as
they're starting to speak,

00:08:34.244 --> 00:08:38.690
it looks like they're retreating,
and therefore maybe nervous or shy.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:43.018
But if somebody actually steps forward, we
have a perception that they're confident

00:08:43.018 --> 00:08:45.440
and stepping into
the challenging situation.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:50.210
So, it's not only what we perceive it's
how the audience perceives it as well.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:50.730
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:54.790
It's might be useful for people to think
about the fact that there's only three

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:57.450
responses we can have to any circumstance.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:01.790
One is to stay still, one is to move
forward, or one is to move back.

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:05.281
Back in 2018, a graduate student
in the neurosciences program,

00:09:05.281 --> 00:09:07.890
who did her thesis with me Lindsay Silay.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:11.405
And I published a paper
in the Journal Nature,

00:09:11.405 --> 00:09:17.550
Lindsay discovered a brain circuit that
controls the movement toward threats.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:19.810
Now, this isn't the kind of
movement that will get you killed.

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:24.132
This is the kind of movement toward in
an intelligent way, in an adaptive way,

00:09:24.132 --> 00:09:28.322
towards something that, in this case
an animal or a person wants to do, but

00:09:28.322 --> 00:09:32.934
feels a tremendous amount of autonomic
arousal of stress and nervousness about.

00:09:32.934 --> 00:09:35.690
And the take home message
is the following,

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:40.461
forward movement under conditions of
anxiety or high levels of alertness,

00:09:40.461 --> 00:09:44.633
aka stress, triggers the activation
of a circuit deep in the brain

00:09:44.633 --> 00:09:47.482
that releases the neurochemical dopamine.

00:09:47.482 --> 00:09:48.945
Dopamine, of course,

00:09:48.945 --> 00:09:53.562
is a molecule that is most commonly
associated with a sensation of reward.

00:09:53.562 --> 00:09:57.988
And it is released when we achieve
something that we want to achieve.

00:09:57.988 --> 00:10:02.655
But the other very interesting
function of dopamine, is to increase

00:10:02.655 --> 00:10:07.974
the probability that we will move toward
similar types of goals in the future.

00:10:07.974 --> 00:10:10.553
So, dopamine is not just
the molecule reward,

00:10:10.553 --> 00:10:13.010
it's the molecule of motivation and drive.

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:17.374
And so, Lindsay's results have
a number of different implications.

00:10:17.374 --> 00:10:21.473
But I think if people can just
conceptualize that the anxiety or

00:10:21.473 --> 00:10:26.499
stress response is the same as the
excitement response, they feel different

00:10:26.499 --> 00:10:31.322
because of some top down perception or
verbiage that we introduced to it.

00:10:31.322 --> 00:10:35.012
But they're actually
identical physiologically and

00:10:35.012 --> 00:10:39.906
that forward movement provided it's
adaptive toward a goal, triggers

00:10:39.906 --> 00:10:45.199
the activation of chemicals in the brain
and body that will make the subsequent

00:10:45.199 --> 00:10:50.197
pursuit of those same or similar goals
more likely and more pleasurable.

00:10:50.197 --> 00:10:51.137
&gt;&gt; That's really cool.

00:10:51.137 --> 00:10:53.786
So learning to take the stage and
step forward,

00:10:53.786 --> 00:10:55.982
leaning in when your virtual can help.

00:10:55.982 --> 00:10:59.400
And I believe in some of
the work of yours that I read,

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.765
you don't actually have
to even physically move.

00:11:02.765 --> 00:11:06.202
If you simulate movement with your eyes,
you can have a similar effect, and

00:11:06.202 --> 00:11:08.620
I'd like for you to talk about that.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.893
&gt;&gt; Back in the early 80s,
someone actually in Palo Alto,

00:11:11.893 --> 00:11:16.530
a woman by the name of Francine Shapiro
is a psychologist developed a technique,

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:19.411
it was actually developed for
trauma treatment.

00:11:19.411 --> 00:11:26.002
She was a psychologist, did some work at
Stanford, but also at a nearby clinic.

00:11:26.002 --> 00:11:31.371
And essentially, she had found
that taking walks was helpful for

00:11:31.371 --> 00:11:33.428
her anxiety and stress.

00:11:33.428 --> 00:11:36.364
Something that everyone now
I think says okay, dab, but

00:11:36.364 --> 00:11:39.554
we always thought that it was
because of movement in the body.

00:11:39.554 --> 00:11:44.077
But she wanted to import some of that
self-induced relaxation to her clinic.

00:11:44.077 --> 00:11:47.549
And she was clever enough to create
this thing that they call EMDR,

00:11:47.549 --> 00:11:50.033
Eye Movement Desensitization Reprocessing,

00:11:50.033 --> 00:11:53.025
which simply involves moving
the eyes from side to side.

00:11:53.025 --> 00:11:55.613
It looks a little goofy,
if you see someone do it.

00:11:55.613 --> 00:11:58.718
But moving the eyes from side to side,
not up or down, but

00:11:58.718 --> 00:12:01.956
side to side eye movement
actually triggers, we now know,

00:12:01.956 --> 00:12:05.358
suppression of the amygdala,
this fear center in the brain.

00:12:05.358 --> 00:12:09.763
And for years, people would ask me about
EMDR, because I'm a vision scientist and

00:12:09.763 --> 00:12:11.050
I work on stress.

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:13.900
And I thought it was totally kooky and
crazy, and I didn't believe any of it.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:18.232
And my response was, in my mind anyway
was sort of, okay, take your EMDR and

00:12:18.232 --> 00:12:20.980
your magic carpet and-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Right.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:25.190
&gt;&gt; And head down to Big Sur and let's
talk later, if you want to talk science.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.585
But I was really quite wrong, because
a couple of years ago, there were no fewer

00:12:29.585 --> 00:12:34.138
than five papers published in very
high-quality journals including, Nature.

00:12:34.138 --> 00:12:38.964
In mice, non-human primates, and
human showing that these lateralized eye

00:12:38.964 --> 00:12:42.927
movements lead to suppression of
this fear center in the brain.

00:12:42.927 --> 00:12:44.762
So it's a quite long lasting effect.

00:12:44.762 --> 00:12:47.877
I should just mention, if people
are going to use it to deal with actual

00:12:47.877 --> 00:12:50.677
trauma, that shouldn't be done
with a real trauma threat.

00:12:50.677 --> 00:12:51.991
&gt;&gt; Sure, right.

00:12:51.991 --> 00:12:56.379
&gt;&gt; It works best for specific
circumstances like public speaking.

00:12:56.379 --> 00:13:00.335
It's not great for sort of reducing your
stress about your entire childhood, or

00:13:00.335 --> 00:13:01.570
your entire-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:04.460
&gt;&gt; Divorce, or your entire 2020.

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:08.307
It's best geared toward specific
circumstances, but here's how it works.

00:13:08.307 --> 00:13:10.758
You move your eyes from side to side for
about 30 seconds,

00:13:10.758 --> 00:13:12.288
which is actually quite a long time.

00:13:12.288 --> 00:13:14.548
You don't do this during the event.

00:13:14.548 --> 00:13:17.134
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; But that creates a state of reduced

00:13:17.134 --> 00:13:21.711
alertness, aka stress, not so
much you fall asleep in your system.

00:13:21.711 --> 00:13:26.170
And then you're able to better
approach things with more ease and

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:27.721
with less alertness.

00:13:27.721 --> 00:13:31.345
&gt;&gt; I've heard you mentioned
on your podcast that there

00:13:31.345 --> 00:13:36.131
are two approaches to dressing stress,
can you share those with us?

00:13:36.131 --> 00:13:39.731
&gt;&gt; The two approaches are you can
either reduce your stress in real time,

00:13:39.731 --> 00:13:43.576
things like this eye movements right
before you go into a stressful event,

00:13:43.576 --> 00:13:48.175
like up on stage, or hard conversation, or
even if you're just experiencing anxiety.

00:13:48.175 --> 00:13:52.475
There's a breathing tool I'll share
with you in a moment that's grounded

00:13:52.475 --> 00:13:54.257
deeply in physiology as well.

00:13:54.257 --> 00:14:00.680
Or you can raise your stress threshold,
so things like ice baths,

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:05.510
running up steep mountains,
exposure therapy.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:10.528
Those are actually designed to increase
your cognitive tolerance for high levels

00:14:10.528 --> 00:14:15.544
of agitation in your body, increasing
the probability that you will stay still,

00:14:15.544 --> 00:14:20.066
not say the wrong thing, not strike
anyone, not lose your cool, right?

00:14:20.066 --> 00:14:23.073
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; So, it's sort of learning to be calm in

00:14:23.073 --> 00:14:28.592
storm or what is sometimes referred to as
being comfortable being uncomfortable.

00:14:28.592 --> 00:14:31.720
That is a distinctly
different set of practices.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:36.221
Then things like the eye movement that
I described, or the second thing,

00:14:36.221 --> 00:14:40.723
which is a real time tool for calming
oneself that My Laboratory is working

00:14:40.723 --> 00:14:45.536
closely on with David Pigott's Laboratory
on the department of psychiatry.

00:14:45.536 --> 00:14:47.387
Which is we ask the question,

00:14:47.387 --> 00:14:51.469
what breathing approach is the best
to calm oneself in real time?

00:14:51.469 --> 00:14:56.552
Because meditation is wonderful, TM is
wonderful, if you're of drinking age and

00:14:56.552 --> 00:15:01.051
you can tolerate it without going into
excess, a nice glass of wine is also

00:15:01.051 --> 00:15:03.894
another way to activate the-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Right.

00:15:03.894 --> 00:15:08.478
&gt;&gt; So is a massage, so
is a dip in the hot tub or a sauna.

00:15:08.478 --> 00:15:11.412
&gt;&gt; Sounds like you're
describing a nice date.

00:15:11.412 --> 00:15:13.911
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Right, yeah, exactly.

00:15:13.911 --> 00:15:18.016
And remember it's called the rest and
digest system for a reason,

00:15:18.016 --> 00:15:22.705
one of the most powerful ways to shift
your autonomic nervous system to one of

00:15:22.705 --> 00:15:25.431
more calmness is to fill
your gut with food.

00:15:25.431 --> 00:15:29.702
That then the distention of your gut
sends signals through the vagus nerve to

00:15:29.702 --> 00:15:34.169
a little clap set of neurons, right behind
your ear called the nodose ganglia,

00:15:34.169 --> 00:15:38.329
which projects into your brain,
gives you a little bit of a dopamine hit.

00:15:38.329 --> 00:15:39.815
This is well established,

00:15:39.815 --> 00:15:44.730
as well as activating areas of the brain
that are involved in calming you down.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:49.874
And so, there are a lot of ways to control
the autonomic nervous system slowly and

00:15:49.874 --> 00:15:54.240
indirectly, massage, hot tub,
big meal, et cetera.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:56.560
We're talking about
real time control fast.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:00.384
So, the best way that we know, and
this is work that's still in progress,

00:16:00.384 --> 00:16:03.120
is to use what are called
physiological sighs.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.810
So, these were discovered back in the 30s.

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:10.209
Turns out that when you are stressed,
you are breathing less deeply.

00:16:10.209 --> 00:16:13.645
The most common advice is
to take a deep breath.

00:16:13.645 --> 00:16:14.348
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:16:14.348 --> 00:16:18.004
&gt;&gt; It turns out that's
exactly the wrong advice.

00:16:18.004 --> 00:16:19.170
&gt;&gt; No.

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:23.669
&gt;&gt; Exhale emphasized breathing leads to
much more rapid activation of the calming

00:16:23.669 --> 00:16:25.910
arm of the nervous system.

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:31.210
And it turns out, you don't just want to
exhale, you want to do a double inhale.

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:33.070
So inhale twice through the nose.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:36.069
So inhale through the nose,
and then before you exhale,

00:16:36.069 --> 00:16:39.510
sneak in a little bit more air,
and then do a long exhale.

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:41.302
And you do this just one to three times.

00:16:41.302 --> 00:16:46.398
So it's inhale, inhale again, even if
you just sneak the tiniest bit of air.

00:16:46.398 --> 00:16:49.960
Ideally, the inhales are done through the
nose, and then exhale through the mouth.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:51.478
Now, why does this work?

00:16:51.478 --> 00:16:55.760
Turns out that your lungs are not
just two big bags of air.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:17:01.630
They are billions of little tiny sacs
called the alveoli of the lungs.

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:02.200
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:06.650
&gt;&gt; Those little sacs are contiguous,
more or less, with the vasculature,

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:08.650
with the blood supply.

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:13.144
So when you exhale, you offload carbon
dioxide, and a lot of the stress response

00:17:13.144 --> 00:17:16.200
is due to elevated carbon
dioxide in the bloodstream.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:21.108
If you've ever been to a kid's party, and
you're asked to blow up a balloon and

00:17:21.108 --> 00:17:22.661
you blow on the balloon.

00:17:22.661 --> 00:17:24.907
Sometimes it inflates right away, but

00:17:24.907 --> 00:17:29.070
if it doesn't, you give two
hard pushes with air, one, two.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:32.008
And the same thing happens
with the alveoli of the lungs.

00:17:32.008 --> 00:17:34.352
As we get stressed,
they start to collapse, they flatten out.

00:17:34.352 --> 00:17:35.624
And to reinflate them,

00:17:35.624 --> 00:17:39.506
the double inhale brings maximal air
into the alveoli of your lungs, and

00:17:39.506 --> 00:17:44.080
then you offload the maximum amount
of carbon dioxide when you exhale.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:45.894
So this is very simple, very fast.

00:17:45.894 --> 00:17:49.630
You can actually do it
during exercise as well.

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:54.272
So if you ever find that your heart is
pounding and you want to calm down,

00:17:54.272 --> 00:17:55.853
first thing is exhale.

00:17:55.853 --> 00:18:00.590
Then maybe try the double inhale,
and then exhale will follow.

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:03.210
Usually, at least what
we find in our studies,

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:07.925
is that within just one to three of those
cycles, meaning within about five seconds,

00:18:07.925 --> 00:18:11.960
autonomic nervous system starts
to shift more towards calmness.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:15.480
And then if you like you can start
using your eye movements or whatever,

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:16.660
cognitive reframing.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:19.857
But one of the things that I
think will resonate with people,

00:18:19.857 --> 00:18:23.821
and I hope it does, is that it is very
hard to control the mind with the mind.

00:18:23.821 --> 00:18:27.355
When you're stepping up to the podium and
you are nervous,

00:18:27.355 --> 00:18:31.525
you can say, yeah, that nervousness
is actually excitement and

00:18:31.525 --> 00:18:34.960
I must be really agitated because
I really want to do this.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:39.420
But in that moment of stress,
it's very hard to control what's going on.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:44.673
So under conditions where your
mind is not where you want it,

00:18:44.673 --> 00:18:47.561
use the body to control the mind.

00:18:47.561 --> 00:18:49.875
&gt;&gt; So you've just shared techniques for

00:18:49.875 --> 00:18:54.100
what we can do when we're
directly experiencing anxiety.

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:57.410
What can we do to prepare for
anxiety in advance?

00:18:58.860 --> 00:19:02.835
&gt;&gt; The other thing to do is the stuff
that you do away from the podium,

00:19:02.835 --> 00:19:05.890
away from the big event or
the hard conversation.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:10.304
And that involves deliberately taking
yourself into states of heightened

00:19:10.304 --> 00:19:12.010
alertness.

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:13.823
As my colleague
David Spiegel likes to say,

00:19:13.823 --> 00:19:17.155
it's not just about the state you find
yourself in, it's how you got there, and

00:19:17.155 --> 00:19:19.350
whether or
not you had anything to do with it.

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:23.995
And what he's really saying is that when
you drive your nervous system into a state

00:19:23.995 --> 00:19:26.769
of high alertness and
you learn to be calm there.

00:19:26.769 --> 00:19:31.287
You achieve a certain kind of superpower
such that when real life puts you into

00:19:31.287 --> 00:19:35.751
those states without any warning and
very fast, it's like driving in fog.

00:19:35.751 --> 00:19:37.950
The first time you do it,
it's scary as heck.

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:41.314
The fifth time to do it,
it's still scary, but

00:19:41.314 --> 00:19:45.697
you've been there before, and
so you're now a good driver.

00:19:45.697 --> 00:19:50.068
So the way you do this can be
of different practices, but

00:19:50.068 --> 00:19:53.324
one of the best ones
is a very cold shower.

00:19:53.324 --> 00:19:56.910
And trying to stay calm for one to
three minutes in a very cold shower.

00:19:56.910 --> 00:19:58.826
&gt;&gt; Wow.
&gt;&gt; Not something that I like,

00:19:58.826 --> 00:20:00.271
it's not very pleasant.

00:20:00.271 --> 00:20:04.691
And there you use a protocol of
breathing that involves taking

00:20:04.691 --> 00:20:09.536
25 to 30 deep inhales, for
reasons that now should be obvious,

00:20:09.536 --> 00:20:13.025
increase your heart rate,
and then big exhale.

00:20:13.025 --> 00:20:17.389
[SOUND]
&gt;&gt; Hyperventilating like.

00:20:17.389 --> 00:20:19.983
&gt;&gt; You hyperventilate.
And by the 25th one,

00:20:19.983 --> 00:20:22.675
you will be very stressed.

00:20:22.675 --> 00:20:25.551
Now I want to say that if people are panic
attack prone or anxiety attack prone,

00:20:25.551 --> 00:20:27.770
I do not recommend this,
very uncomfortable.

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:31.800
But then what you do at the end of that
25 breaths is you offload all your air,

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:32.980
you empty your lungs.

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:37.782
[SOUND] And then you sit for
about 15 to, some people can go longer,

00:20:37.782 --> 00:20:40.237
60 seconds or so, lungs empty.

00:20:40.237 --> 00:20:41.057
And you try and

00:20:41.057 --> 00:20:45.108
feel peaceful with that heightened
level of adrenaline in your body.

00:20:45.108 --> 00:20:49.350
Now, never do this near water, I want to
be really clear because there is this

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:52.862
thing called shallow water, but
don't even do it in a puddle,

00:20:52.862 --> 00:20:56.001
because I don't want anyone
injuring themselves or worse.

00:20:56.001 --> 00:21:00.058
But if you repeat that for two or
three cycles, what you will find,

00:21:00.058 --> 00:21:01.734
it's pretty remarkable.

00:21:01.734 --> 00:21:06.261
There's some nuance to these practices,
but they all kind of start and

00:21:06.261 --> 00:21:09.000
end with vision or breathing.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:12.162
&gt;&gt; Right, and I love how you make
them simple for us to understand.

00:21:12.162 --> 00:21:15.779
So they're the things we
can do in the moment, or

00:21:15.779 --> 00:21:18.788
right before the moment of the anxiety.

00:21:18.788 --> 00:21:21.097
And then they're things we can do to, in
essence, desensitize ourselves in advance.

00:21:21.097 --> 00:21:25.043
Now, we are all communicating in
this virtual world these days.

00:21:25.043 --> 00:21:30.007
Is there anything from your experience
of how eyes work that would indicate

00:21:30.007 --> 00:21:31.280
what we should do?

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:33.951
For example, people often say,
you need to look at the camera so

00:21:33.951 --> 00:21:36.485
it looks like you're looking at
the person on the other end.

00:21:36.485 --> 00:21:42.500
Any insight you have about this
virtual communication we all do?

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:46.029
&gt;&gt; Okay, well, first of all,
it's a very unnatural time,

00:21:46.029 --> 00:21:50.424
most of all because we are not used to
seeing a little picture of ourselves

00:21:50.424 --> 00:21:52.746
moving while we look at other people.

00:21:52.746 --> 00:21:55.965
Everyone's carrying a little mirror
around on their shoulder for

00:21:55.965 --> 00:21:57.007
us now in this world.

00:21:57.007 --> 00:22:00.037
So turn that off, or
get it out of the way,

00:22:00.037 --> 00:22:05.061
because it will interfere with your
presence to the conversation and

00:22:05.061 --> 00:22:09.310
other people's perception
of how present you are.

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:11.969
The other thing that's
really important is,

00:22:11.969 --> 00:22:16.553
there's a lot of research on gaze and
eye contact, and frequency of blinking.

00:22:16.553 --> 00:22:20.977
The most powerful way to connect
with somebody through Zoom or

00:22:20.977 --> 00:22:26.540
just in person, is actually not to
stare directly at them the whole time.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:32.349
Is a combination of direct gaze,
averting gaze, and closing one's eyes.

00:22:32.349 --> 00:22:37.220
A real conversation involves moments
whereyoue're looking away trying to,

00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:40.760
like I'm doing right now,
I'm trying grab a concept and

00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:43.050
say it in a way that makes sense.

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:44.649
But then also where we re-engage.

00:22:44.649 --> 00:22:48.520
And so a conversation is actually
a process of looking directly at the other

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:51.910
person and then breaking gaze and
then coming back again.

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:56.496
The other thing that works quite well if
people are experiencing eye fatigue from

00:22:56.496 --> 00:23:01.419
looking at screens up close, and there's
a whole other conversation, one thing that

00:23:01.419 --> 00:23:05.211
works well is to see someone's
face at the beginning, say hello.

00:23:05.211 --> 00:23:09.345
Because faces are a powerful,
collects a lot of context for the brain.

00:23:09.345 --> 00:23:10.633
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; Saying hello, but

00:23:10.633 --> 00:23:14.322
then going into pure audio, and then going
back to visual before you part ways.

00:23:14.322 --> 00:23:17.926
That may actually be a more effective form
of Zoom communication than pretending

00:23:17.926 --> 00:23:21.424
we're all in the same room and trying to
stare at one another the entire time and

00:23:21.424 --> 00:23:23.493
sometimes see that
reflection of ourselves.

00:23:23.493 --> 00:23:27.264
&gt;&gt; Right, and that's advice that
others on this podcast have given,

00:23:27.264 --> 00:23:30.720
that you don't have to show
your video the whole time.

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:33.872
So before we end, Andrew, I'd like to
ask you the same three questions I ask

00:23:33.872 --> 00:23:35.555
everybody who joins me on this podcast.

00:23:35.555 --> 00:23:36.295
Are you up for that?

00:23:36.295 --> 00:23:38.685
&gt;&gt; Definitely.

00:23:38.685 --> 00:23:39.771
&gt;&gt; If you were to capture the best
communication advice you've ever received,

00:23:39.771 --> 00:23:40.768
as a five to seven word presentation
slide title, what would that be?

00:23:56.074 --> 00:24:01.393
&gt;&gt; Speak to inform, and
teach, not impress.

00:24:01.393 --> 00:24:01.921
&gt;&gt; I like that.

00:24:01.921 --> 00:24:03.684
So it's about getting
the information across.

00:24:03.684 --> 00:24:05.734
And you are certainly a master at that.

00:24:05.734 --> 00:24:09.616
And I encourage everybody To search
out some of the work you've done,

00:24:09.616 --> 00:24:12.939
your podcast is fantastic because
you do such a nice job of something

00:24:12.939 --> 00:24:17.520
we talked about on an earlier podcast, how
to make complex information accessible.

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:18.690
You do a great job of that.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:22.772
You certainly don't dumb it down, but
you make it accessible as you've done for

00:24:22.772 --> 00:24:23.750
us today.

00:24:23.750 --> 00:24:28.284
Lemme ask question number two, who is
a communication that you admire and why?

00:24:38.911 --> 00:24:42.956
&gt;&gt; There two forms of communication
that really appealed to me and

00:24:42.956 --> 00:24:45.796
the they will seem at
odds with one another.

00:24:45.796 --> 00:24:51.955
I love poetry because great poetry doesn't

00:24:51.955 --> 00:24:58.150
really make sense at a cognitive level.

00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:03.530
It gets to a kind of what probably is
some sort of deeper, universal meaning.

00:25:03.530 --> 00:25:08.098
It probably is tapping into
brain circuits that are more on

00:25:08.098 --> 00:25:13.360
a emotional level or
that reside in the brain body connection.

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:18.290
I really believe that so that speaks
to some sort of quote unquote truth.

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:23.290
So I love poetry and
I'm a big Wendell Berry fan.

00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:27.542
I'm also a big Joe Strummer fan, most
famous for being the singer the clash for

00:25:27.542 --> 00:25:30.390
the things he said Not
while he was on stage.

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:35.450
So Joe Strummer is brilliant in
terms of his offstage speech, for

00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:40.602
people to check out some of the things
that Joe said later in his life

00:25:40.602 --> 00:25:45.760
were really remarkable insights
into human beings and humanity.

00:25:45.760 --> 00:25:49.725
There's some real core truth there.

00:25:49.725 --> 00:25:55.800
And in the world of science
Claude Despawn is a Biology

00:25:55.800 --> 00:26:01.530
professor at NYU who
studies insect vision.

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:06.198
And the only word that I can use to
describe what it is to hear one of

00:26:06.198 --> 00:26:08.532
Claude's lecture, whether or

00:26:08.532 --> 00:26:13.127
not you're familiar with Biology or
not is pure enchantment.

00:26:13.127 --> 00:26:17.843
He transports you into a world
where frankly I don't really care

00:26:17.843 --> 00:26:21.162
about dragonfly vision
on a regular basis but

00:26:21.162 --> 00:26:26.760
when I listen to Claude speak I think
about human vision I think about love.

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:30.696
He talks about the love spots
of the dragon fly eyes thats for

00:26:30.696 --> 00:26:34.550
pursuing meats and food and
it's just remarkable.

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:39.519
And anyone that can do
that is incredible but

00:26:39.519 --> 00:26:44.490
Claude is the world
heavyweight champion of

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:49.747
making Biology fascinating and delightful.

00:26:49.747 --> 00:26:52.280
&gt;&gt; Well, I'm going to check
his lectures out for sure.

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:53.770
Last question for you.

00:26:53.770 --> 00:26:59.240
What are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:26:59.240 --> 00:27:03.450
&gt;&gt; Passion,
the speaker has to love the topic.

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:06.469
In organizational logic,

00:27:06.469 --> 00:27:11.726
there has to be a structure
to the information.

00:27:11.726 --> 00:27:16.780
It just can't be bullet points and
beginning middle end kind of thing.

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:18.650
And clarity.

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:24.792
If people walk away understanding
more than they did at the beginning,

00:27:24.792 --> 00:27:29.192
then you won, get back to the the most
important thing to do is to

00:27:29.192 --> 00:27:32.330
teach your audience educate them.

00:27:32.330 --> 00:27:34.520
&gt;&gt; Passion, structure, and clarity, PSC.

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:38.296
We're going to add that as another acronym
to your list of many acronyms I've heard

00:27:38.296 --> 00:27:39.570
you talk about.

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:42.000
Andrew, it was fantastic to have you here.

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:45.717
I'm taking a deep breath to calm myself
down from all the exciting information

00:27:45.717 --> 00:27:47.890
that you shared, thank you so much.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:52.279
It is my true hope that all of us
listening in can apply some of these tools

00:27:52.279 --> 00:27:55.655
and hacks to optimize our communication,
thank you.

00:27:55.655 --> 00:27:57.330
&gt;&gt; Thanks so much for having me.

